From u373beb702917b8b5 at groups.google.com  Tue Jun  3 00:51:32 2014
From: u373beb702917b8b5 at groups.google.com (Ista Zahn)
Date: Tue, 03 Jun 2014 00:51:32 +0000
Subject: Re: stat_density Produces Incorrect Kernel Density Estimates
Message-ID: <0N96zJXxtLE.YxA3TDQkgy0J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <0N96zJXxtLE.ifi_PTC2TToJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <0N96zJXxtLE.ifi_PTC2TToJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/0N96zJXxtLE/m/YxA3TDQkgy0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The area under the curves are in fact the same, but those areas are
stacked on top of one another. This makes sense for the default
geom_area, but not for geom_path; when using the path geom you should
also change the position adjustment, e.g.,

ggplot(plotData,
       aes(x = expr, colour = groups)) +
  stat_density(aes(y = ..density..), geom = "path", size = 1, adjust = 0.5) +

  scale_x_continuous(limits = c(0, 16))

or take the easy way ant use geom_density() instead of stat_density():

ggplot(plotData,
       aes(x = expr, color = groups)) +
  geom_density(adjust=0.5) +
  scale_x_continuous(limits = c(0, 16))

Best,
Ista

